X-BEDEWORK-COST:Free
CREATED:20260913T201324Z
DESCRIPTION:Photographer and Duke MFA|EDA alumna Cassandra Klos explores h
 umanity's relationship to space\, scientific exploration\, and the possib
 ility of life beyond Earth in "Cosmic Origins\," an exhibition on view at
  CDS October 22 - January 4\, 2026.\n\n** Join us for the opening recepti
 on at CDS on Thursday\, October 22\, from 5:30 - 7 p.m. Come back for a f
 ree family event on Saturday\, October 24\, 11 a.m. - 3 p.m. **\n\nGALLER
 Y HOURS\nMonday - Thursday: 9 am - 5 pm.\nFriday: 9 am - 4 pm.\n\nBringin
 g together photographs\, artifacts\, and ephemera from simulated space mi
 ssions\, the exhibition features work from Klos's long-term project "Mars
  on Earth\," which documents her experiences as an analog astronaut parti
 cipating in Earth-based simulations designed to replicate the conditions\
 , challenges\, and isolation of space exploration.\n\nWith pressurized sp
 acesuits\, freeze-dried food\, robotic companions\, and Mission Control w
 aiting back at "home\," Klos's photographs visualize what life beyond Ear
 th might look and feel like for the first cosmic explorers. The exhibitio
 n also includes spacesuits and materials from Klos's fieldwork\, as well 
 as work originally produced for National Geographic that will be exhibite
 d together in the CDS Kreps Gallery for the first time.\n\nBlurring the b
 oundaries between documentary evidence and speculative imagination\, "Cos
 mic Origins" invites visitors to consider not only how humans might live 
 on the Moon or Mars\, but also what space exploration can teach us about 
 life on Earth.\n\nMore info about the exhibition: https://documentarystud
 ies.duke.edu/upcoming-exhibitions. Organized by CDS in collaboration with
  Duke University's MFA|EDA program and the Duke SPACE Initiative\, with s
 upport from Duke's Office of Interdisciplinary Programs.
