CREATED:20261006T150328Z
DESCRIPTION:Duke English and the Duke Writers Collective invite you to a b
 ilingual poetry reading at Duke Coffee House on Saturday\, October 24th\,
  from 7-8 PM at the Duke Coffee House. Words and Motion: Poetry in Transl
 ation brings writers Tsitsi Jaji and Crystal Simone Smith together with p
 oet-translator duo Claire Cullen and Joann Woo.  Moving among languages\,
  histories\, and forms\, the night's readers will consider writing not on
 ly as something that can be translated\, but as an act of translation its
 elf-a bridge between experience\, place\, and mind.  Light refreshments s
 erved. \nBios:\nClaire Cullen and Joann Woo are the co-presidents of the 
 Duke Writers' Collective (DWC)\, a close community of writers at Duke Uni
 versity where writers meet\, share\, and grow in their personal creative 
 endeavors. Along with snacks\, DWC offers weekly writing hours\, communit
 y-building events\, and outreach workshops in Durham public schools. \n \
 nCrystal Simone Smith is the author of several collections of poetry\, Co
 mmon Sense (1776)\, Addressed to the Citizens of Today: An Erasure (Beaco
 n Press\, 2026)\, Runagate: Songs of the Freedom Bound (Duke University P
 ress\, 2025)\, winner of the Roanoke-Chowan Poetry Award\, and Dark Testa
 ment (Henry Holt\, 2023). In 2022\, her haiku collection\, Ebbing Shore\,
  won The Haiku Foundation Touchstone Distinguished Book Award. Smith is t
 he recipient of a Duke Humanities Unbounded Fellowship. Her work has appe
 ared in numerous journals including POETRY Magazine\, Harper's Magazine\,
  Rattle\, Frogpond\, and Modern Haiku. She teaches in the Thompson Writin
 g Program at Duke University and writes poetry about the human condition 
 and social change.\n \nTsitsi Jaji teaches English and African & African 
 American Studies at Duke University\, where she is Helen L. Bevington Ass
 ociate Professor of Modern Poetry. She is a senior research associate at 
 Rhodes University. Her books include the award-winning monograph Africa i
 n Stereo: Music\, Modernism and Pan-African Solidarity\, two volumes of p
 oetry\, Mother Tongues and Beating the Graves\, and the chapbook Carnaval
 . Several of her poems have been set to music\, and her collaboration wit
 h Shawn Okpebholo\, Songs in Flight\, was nominated for a 2025 Grammy. Sh
 e specializes in not quite making it\, but she's happy being a runner-up.
 
DURATION:PT1H
