CREATED:20260729T151625Z
DESCRIPTION:Over the last four years\, the Shia Islamic-Jewish Legal Reaso
 ning in Dialogue has brought together 13 scholars from across the U.S. an
 d internationally to study legal texts of both traditions via diverse met
 hodologies of academic analysis. They have been divided  into 6 working g
 roups working on the topics of the legal self\, constitutionalism\, theoc
 racy\, language\, female clerical authority\, and the persecution of here
 tics. The Dialogue is not merely meant to identify similarities and diffe
 rences\, but to allow scholars to think their "own" tradition conceptuall
 y anew through this encounter. The project arrives at Duke in order to pr
 esent its findings and receive constructive critique from Triangle-area s
 cholars of co-written book chapters that will eventually be published in 
 Rutgers University Press' "Transmodern Times" series.\n\nMore information
  at: \nhttps://jewishstudies.duke.edu/shia-islamic-jewish-legal-reasoning
 -dialogue \n\nSchedule:\n\nSunday:  October 25th\n\n8:30-9:15. Welcomes\,
  Introductions \n\n9:15-11:00. Session 1: Hermeneutics and Language\n\n11
 :15-1:00. Session 2: Theocracy\n\n1:00-2:00. Lunch\n \n2:00- 3:45. Sessio
 n 3: Key Concepts of Clerical Authority \n\n4:00-5:45. Session 4: Female 
 Clerical Authority\n\n\nMonday:  October 26th\n\n8:30- 10:15. Session 5: 
 Identifying and Punishing the Apostate\n\n10:30-12:15 Session 6: The Lega
 l Self\n\n12:15-1:00. Lunch \n\n1:00-2:45. Session 7: Constitutionalism \
 n\n3:00-4:00  Concluding Thoughts and Questions
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261026T180000
