CONTACT;X-BEDEWORK-UID=8a0183a7-83184018-0184-87170e31-00006277:Hinz\, Nic
 olle
CREATED:20261012T183949Z
DESCRIPTION:This talk presents a body of work aimed at closing that gap\, 
 premised on the idea that a sharper physical understanding of how porous 
 solids fail is what enables good approximations of it. Mechanical stress 
 in a loaded microstructure concentrates at the geometric constrictions of
  the solid\, which we hypothesize to be the preferred sites for cracks to
  nucleate under most loads. This is the basis for a reduced-order model t
 hat foreshadows where cracks form and thereby gains several orders of mag
 nitude in computational speed. This hypothesis\, which was first supporte
 d by phase-field simulations\, is now being confirmed by in-situ tensile 
 experiments on 3D-printed porous specimens. Underlying these approximatio
 ns is a pore-level multiscale method (PLMM) that decomposes the solid int
 o subdomains with local\, physics-based closures. We discuss a geometric 
 formulation of it that allows cracks to take arbitrary trajectories\, ove
 rcoming the limitations of the reduced-order model. We then recast PLMM a
 s an algebraic preconditioner\, so it accelerates existing finite-element
  and finite-volume codes by over an order of magnitude relative to algebr
 aic multigrid. The preconditioner retains rigorous error control and hand
 les both pre-existing and evolving cracks. We also discuss a high-order v
 ariant that captures bending- and torsion-dominated loads\, the use of ma
 chine learning to lower the cost of building the preconditioner\, and ong
 oing extensions to plasticity and large-strain deformations. PLMM origina
 ted in pore-scale flow\, exploiting the constrictions of the void space t
 o outperform pore-network models. Only recently have the above ideas for 
 mechanics enabled its geometric formulation to be recast as a preconditio
 ner for the Navier-Stokes equations. The talk weighs trade-offs of these 
 accelerations and points to current gaps\, including fluid-induced failur
 e and upscaling to Darcy scale.
DURATION:PT1H
DTSTAMP:20261012T183949Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261026T120000
LAST-MODIFIED:20261012T183949Z
